    quiz: [
      { id: 1, question: 'What is the main difference between localStorage and sessionStorage?', options: ['localStorage is faster', 'localStorage persists across sessions, sessionStorage is per-tab', 'localStorage has more capacity', 'sessionStorage is encrypted'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'localStorage persists data until explicitly cleared. sessionStorage is scoped to the current browser tab and cleared when the tab closes.' },
      { id: 2, question: 'Which storage option is best for storing large structured datasets?', options: ['localStorage', 'Cookies', 'IndexedDB', 'Session storage'], correctIndex: 2, explanation: 'IndexedDB supports large datasets (hundreds of MB), structured data with indexes, and transactions — ideal for complex client-side storage.' },
      { id: 3, question: 'What is the storage limit for localStorage?', options: ['No limit', '5MB per origin', '100KB per domain', '64KB total'], correctIndex: 1, explanation: 'localStorage has a ~5MB limit per origin (protocol + domain + port). This limit varies slightly between browsers but is typically 5-10MB.' }
    ],
    topics: [
      {
        id: 'local-session', title: 'localStorage and sessionStorage',
        content: 'localStorage and sessionStorage provide key-value string storage with the Web Storage API. localStorage persists data across browser sessions — it survives tab closures, browser restarts, and page reloads. sessionStorage is scoped to the current tab — data is cleared when the tab closes. Both share the same API: getItem(key), setItem(key, value), removeItem(key), clear(), key(index). Data is stored as strings — use JSON.stringify() and JSON.parse() for objects. Both are synchronous and block the main thread. Limit: ~5MB per origin (protocol + domain + port). Data is shared across same-origin tabs — changes in one tab trigger Storage events in others (localStorage only). Never store sensitive data (tokens, passwords) — storage is accessible to any JavaScript on the page. Angular patterns: create a StorageService that abstracts the API, handles JSON serialization, and provides type safety: getItem<T>(key: string): T | null. Use for user preferences, UI state, and non-sensitive cache data.'
      },
      {
        id: 'indexeddb', title: 'IndexedDB',
        content: 'IndexedDB is a transactional, asynchronous database for large structured data. It supports storing objects, arrays, Blobs, and any structured-clonable data. Schema is defined through object stores (tables) with indexes for efficient querying. Opening a database: const request = indexedDB.open("MyDB", 1); request.onupgradeneeded defines the schema. Transaction modes: readonly (default), readwrite, and versionchange. Object stores support put (insert/update), get (by key), delete, getAll, and cursor-based iteration. Indexes enable efficient queries: store.createIndex("email", "email", { unique: true }). IndexedDB is asynchronous and event-driven — no async/await without a Promise wrapper. Libraries like idb (by Jake Archibald) provide a Promise-based API. Angular patterns: create an IndexedDBService using the idb library for type-safe operations. Capacity: much larger than localStorage (hundreds of MB to GB, varies by browser). Use IndexedDB for offline data, large caches, and complex queries. Chrome DevTools Application panel provides IndexedDB inspection and editing.'
      },
      {
        id: 'cache-api', title: 'Cache API',
        content: 'The Cache API stores Request/Response pairs, primarily used by service workers for offline caching. Unlike other storage APIs, Cache stores entire HTTP responses including headers and body. Cache instances are named: await caches.open("my-cache-v1"). Store responses: cache.put(request, response). Retrieve: const response = await cache.match(request). The Cache API is asynchronous and Promise-based. Common patterns: cache-first (serve from cache, fallback to network), network-first (try network, fallback to cache), stale-while-revalidate (serve cache, update in background). Cache storage is separate from browser cache — you have full control over what is cached and when. Angular Service Workers use the Cache API internally for PWA caching strategies defined in ngsw-config.json. Cache names should be versioned (v1, v2) — old caches are cleaned up in the service worker activate event. Cache storage is queryable: caches.keys() returns all cache names, cache.keys() returns all stored requests. Storage limits are generous but vary by browser — typically at least 50MB, often much more.'
      },
      {
        id: 'cookies', title: 'Cookies',
        content: 'Cookies are small text data stored by the browser and sent with every HTTP request to the domain that set them. They serve multiple purposes: session management (session IDs), personalization (preferences), and tracking (analytics). Cookie attributes control behavior: expires/max-age sets lifetime, path limits scope, domain extends scope to subdomains, secure requires HTTPS, HttpOnly prevents JavaScript access (document.cookie), and SameSite controls cross-site behavior (Strict, Lax, None). SameSite=Strict prevents sending cookies with cross-site requests (most secure but breaks legitimate cross-site navigation). SameSite=Lax allows cookies with top-level navigation (GET requests from other sites). SameSite=None requires Secure — allows cross-site requests for embedded content. Size limit: ~4KB per cookie, ~50 cookies per domain. In Angular, HttpOnly cookies are automatically sent with HttpClient requests when withCredentials: true is set. Cookies are the only storage mechanism that survives all navigation types and is sent automatically — this makes them essential for authentication but also vulnerable to CSRF attacks.'
      }
    ]
