        content: 'The Memory panel identifies memory leaks and high memory usage. Heap Snapshots capture the state of JavaScript objects at a point in time. Take a snapshot, perform actions, take another snapshot, and compare — objects that only appear in the later snapshot are potential leaks. The Summary view groups objects by constructor name with count and size. The Comparison view shows differences between two snapshots — objects added (red), removed (blue), or changed. The Containment view shows the object graph starting from GC roots. The Statistics view shows total memory by category. Allocation Timeline records memory allocations over time — spikes indicate heavy allocations. Look for detached DOM elements — they remain in memory if JavaScript holds references. Retainers show why objects are not garbage collected — follow the chain to find the reference keeping them alive. Common Angular leaks: subscriptions not unsubscribed, event listeners not removed, destroyed components still referenced. Force garbage collection (trash icon) before snapshots to get accurate results. Use "Allocation instrumentation on timeline" to see what code allocates the most memory.'
